  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nHeat has returned across the area today, as upper ridging over the \nFour Corners area spreads eastward. This upper ridge will continue \nto shift east over the next day before becoming locked in place over \nthe central CONUS the rest of the week. Meanwhile west of the ridge, \na deep upper low over the western CONUS will allow for lee troughing \nto develop across the High Plains. This will keep the 850 mb thermal \nridge axis in place across the area for about the next week. Mixing \nwill be a bit deeper than some of our prior episodes of heat this \nsummer, so while temperatures will reach the mid/upper 90s (possibly \n100 in spots) each afternoon, dewpoints will stay in the 60s. \nNevertheless, we may need a Heat Advisory at some point, as current \nheat index forecasts are in the 100 to 105 range each day through \nThursday. Considered issuing one this afternoon, but will wait one \nmore forecast cycle to see how deep moisture mixes out this \nafternoon across central Kansas. If dewpoints stay on the higher end\nof guidance through this afternoon, I'll be more confident that we \nreach that 105 heat index threshold for an advisory tomorrow. \n\nWith persistent lee troughing, a decent pressure gradient across the \nPlains will also keep breezy southerly winds in place each day. This \nwill be a double edged sword, as the stronger afternoon winds will \nmake the heat slightly more tolerable, but the continued overnight \nmixing will prevent temperatures from cooling off as much. Low \ntemperatures look to stay mostly in the upper 70s, near record warm \nvalues.\n\nAs upper ridging stays in control across the area, most of the next \nweek will stay dry. Any more notable shortwaves will stay well to our\nnorth. However with a strong low-level jet centered a bit too our \nwest, it's not completely out of the question we could see a few \nscattered showers or storms sneak into far north-central KS. This \nwould be most likely during the overnight or morning hours, if any \nconvection can develop from any stronger isentropic ascent within the\nLLJ. No one day with any more notable signal, but the pattern \nsuggests it can't be ruled out.",
